PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.7/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a professionally built, uniquely solvable 120-level set. Every sampled intent matches its board: I checked clue arithmetic on about 25 levels (L4, L9, L12, L21, L29, L30, L31, L40, L46, L49 and others) and found only one text error, in L63. Chapter themes are clear and each chapter has a breather and a masterwork finale. The measured curve fits the studio targets: L1-3 are 100/100/93% casual, the last third averages about 63% casual, skilled stays between 86% and 100%, and there are never more than 2 easy levels in a row. The faults are these. (1) Several 'mechanics' (reach, deep-chain) are labels on ordinary line logic, so their chapters have no new idea to teach. (2) Some pictures are unreadable (L7, L9, L13, L21, L51, L55), which weakens the paint-a-picture payoff. (3) The stamp is decorative in a few levels (L53, L70). (4) L96 is a near-duplicate of L93. (5) The pacing is predictable, with a breather at every 7th level of every chapter. (6) Some early practice levels waste board space. The set is solid and polished but rarely surprising, so it is below Picross S and Nonogram.com at their best. Curve: Casual win rate falls smoothly from 100% (L1-2) to about 54-60% (L84, L96, L108, L120), with a breather every 12 levels (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115). The 2-easy-in-a-row cap is respected, and L25-26 (91%, 90%) are the only back-to-back easy pair. Late-third average is about 63% casual with skilled 86-100%, which is on target. Mechanic introduction order is good: black-and-white logic (L1-24), then 2 colours (L25-36), then voids (L37-48), then stamps (L49-60), then 3 colours (L61-72), then smudged (L73-84), then cracked (L85-96), then 'deep-chain' (L97-108), then combos (L109-120). The weaknesses are these. (a) Chapter 2 (reach) and chapter 8 (deep-chain) teach no distinct rule, so the first levels of those chapters feel like more of the same. (b) Stamps are mostly hints rather than a new rule. (c) The cadence is predictable: every chapter is teach, teach, practise, practise, twist, boss, breather, twist, practise, combine, twist, finale. (d) Chapters 1-2 are 24 black-and-white levels before the first colour, which is long for a casual audience. (e) The final chapter pairs mechanics already taught, with nothing new at the end. A cross-chapter mastery level such as a smudged+cracked+void board would be a more fitting last test than another colour-cross.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L3 (7/10): At 5x6 the overlap pays off in only one column and the jellyfish is barely readable; the 'aha' is small. → Use a 7x6 with a 5 in 7 row so the overlap spans three columns and a clear drawing.
L4 (7/10): Right two-thirds of the board is empty above the rock; the lighthouse is a thin tower on the left with wasted space. → Centre the tower or add rocks, a beam and a gull on the right to use the 9 columns.
L7 (7/10): Intent is true but 'Sand Bucket' reads as scattered blocks; the picture is not recognisable. → Redraw with a tapering bucket body and a separate handle arc so it reads at a glance.
L9 (7/10): Anchor silhouette is noisy and not recognisable; the flukes and shaft read as random ink. → Redraw with a clear ring, vertical shaft and crescent flukes.
L13 (7/10): 'Reach' is just ordinary line logic, and Toadstools is an unclear picture with a stray left block. → Redraw as two clear mushrooms of different heights so the grow-from-floor logic is the visible lesson.
L21 (7/10): The bear silhouette is hard to read, so the finish has no payoff; the clue is good but the art is mush. → Redraw a clear bear: round ears, snout notch and four legs, keeping the 1 8 column.
L51 (7/10): Right half of the seahorse is mostly empty and the shape is unclear; only 6 lines open. → Redraw the seahorse with a curled tail and snout across the whole grid and keep the column 2 3 2 1.
L53 (7/10): The stamped paper cell likely duplicates what rows 6-8 already force, so the stamp is not essential; the aha is row 4's exact fit. → Move the stamp to a cell no line settles by itself or drop it and call this an exact-fit level.
L55 (7/10): Sparse, scattered ink makes the cat unrecognisable, and only 5 lines open at the start is not breather-calm even with 90% casual wins. → Add a head outline and ears to give about 8 open lines and a readable winking cat.
L63 (7/10): The intent text is wrong: columns 6 and 7 are not 'gold 2, umber, gold 1, sage 4'; column 6 is gold 2, umber 5, gold 1, sage 4. → Correct the intent and flag the umber disc as the central deduction.
L70 (7/10): At 79% casual it is easier than neighbours as a 'twist', and the stamped slit is redundant with the column 8 fill. → Move the stamp to a leaf-slit cell no line forces alone, or tag the level as a combine.
L81 (7/10): Casual 56% is the lowest of its chapter so far while its stated fix is a single stamp, which makes it feel like a hint, not a deduction. → Remove the stamp and supply uniqueness through one more row crossing, or lower the grid width.
L85 (7/10): The ? resolves to the full row (7), so the first cracked clue can be guessed without logic; 77% casual for a teach. → Choose a partial run so the column logic bounds it, e.g. a 4 in 7.
L96 (7/10): It is a near-repeat of L93: same 14x14 3-colour kiln with a flame core and a cracked column 7 #2; finale does not test anything new. → Replace with a new picture and a two-crack crossing, one row and one column on the same hub.
L97 (7/10): 'Deep-chain' is not a distinct mechanic, so this teach has no new rule. → Rename or make it a genuine long-chain level and trim the open-line count to 3-4.

